  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM EST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nCurrently, upper low is moving into the northeast United States with \nthe cyclonic flow remaining over the southern Appalachians and East \nTennessee. Boundary layer southwest flow over the region is \nproducing some low-level isentropic lift in the area and resulting \nlight rain/drizzle and light snow higher terrain. This lift will \ncontinue into the evening hours. However, as the flow weakens as \nupper low lifts northeast and surface ridging building, isentropic \nlift will end along with light precipitation.\n\nFor Tonight, main question is how soon will low clouds dissipate. \nGiven the low-level moisture and typical NBM eroding of clouds too \nearly, we call for a slower clearing from southwest to northeast \nthrough Saturday morning. For areas that do clear, patchy areas of \nfog are possible.\n\nFor Saturday, upper ridging will build into the area along with \nsurface ridging. Warm 850mb temperatures and return of some \nsunshine will allow for milder temperatures.\n\nFor Sunday into Sunday evening, a strengthening upper trough with \n300mb winds increasing from 120 to 145 knots during the period. The \nupper jet will also strengthen the fronto-genetic forcing with the \napproaching front. Widespread showers incoming for Sunday exiting \nSunday night. Ensembles and deterministic models show some elevated \ninstability generally south of interstate 40 so isolated embedded \nthunder is expected. Ensemble QPF along with WPC depicts QPF amounts \nfrom 0.5 to 0.75 inch of most locations.\n\nThe jet dynamics will also strengthen a southerly boundary layer \njet. A 850mb jet of 50-55 knots is expected which will likely \nproduce a mountain wave event for the east Tennessee mountains and \nfoothills. REFS and Mountain Wave Dashboard depict advisory winds \nwith gusts of 50 mph.  Would not be surprised is Cove Mountain has \ngusts in excess of 65 mph. A high wind watch has been issued. \n\nElsewhere, tightening pressure gradient associated with frontal \nboundary will likely produce breezy conditions for Sunday afternoon \nand evening.\n\nFor Monday through Thursday, ensemble cluster analysis shows surface \nand upper ridging will produce mainly dry conditions and a warming \ntrend. Highs will climb back into the 60s with temperatures around \n10 degrees above normal by mid to late week.\n\nFor late next week, another storm system may approach the Tennessee \nvalley and southern Appalachians.",
